hi there steve kaufman here to talk  about languages and today i'm going to  talk about grammar exercises  but before i do that if you enjoy my  videos please subscribe if you have any  particular requests please let me know  i've seen some people would like me to  do a video in spanish or in japanese and  so forth and so i will do that but today  i'm going to talk about grammar  exercises  french grammar exercises spanish grammar  exercises english grammar exercises  every time you buy a book you know to  learn a language  it's got a lot of grammar exercises  there it seems that anyone producing a  textbook  feels that they have to produce these  exercises to drill you and train you in  whatever grammatical rule they've been  trying to teach because sort of together  with the grammar exercises you have  grammar rules grammar explanations  um i don't do grammar exercises i don't  think that the time and stress involved  in trying to think and trying to get the  right answer and then looking to see if  i got the right answer i've never found  that it helped me very much i found that  the time that i spent just with the  language listening and reading and on  link saving words and phrases and  occasionally reviewing these words and  phrases was more useful  i also found that  reviewing and i've said this before many  examples  of structures in the language is useful  again it's it's a review it's an  exposure to the language and sometimes i  will look at just the answers to these  exercises because there you'll tend to  see  because the exercises tend to focus on a  particular grammatical point you'll see  a number of examples of of correct usage  but exercises per se i don't do  and and what i have found is very  interesting i noticed this as you know  i'm working on my korean and my polish  and then i listen to my ukrainian and  and russian and so forth  those languages that i have studied at  link where i actually have an accurate  statistic on how many words i know  it's not the case for languages that i  learned before link like french or  spanish and so forth but those languages  that i've learned essentially at link  russian czech ukrainian romanian korean  polish  what i find is  that my ability to understand the  language is directly is determined by  the statistic how many words i know  and the statistic of how many words i  know at link  is really  a reflection of how much time i have  spent reading and i always listen to the  same content so reading and listening to  the language so  in fact that statistic how many words i  know  reflects how much time i have spent with  the language  not doing exercises but reading and  listening  and so when i am at a level of let's say  30 000 words known words  at length the way we count them where we  count each form of the word as a  different word i'm still struggling  romanian korean uh even polish whereas  when i'm over the forty thousand fifty  thousand hump ukrainian and czech i  understand them a lot better and of  course russian where i'm over like 80 90  000 words i understand russian unless  there's words in there that i don't know  the occasional word by and large i  understand uh you know 90 of what  they're saying so  all to say that that to me language is  about words  and exposure  spending the time learning words  and grammar exercises i don't think to  me they don't even constitute sort of  time spent with the language time spent  with the languages is listening reading  speaking writing but doing exercises to  me  is is not an effective activity and yet  it's the core of most textbooks and most  classroom activities so i'm sure that  will uh solicit some uh criticism from  uh those of you who are watching but i  thought i would get that out there okay  thanks for listening bye for now
 

